\chapter{Networking: Ethernet, ARP, IP, ICMP, UDP, TCP, DHCP and DNS}
\label{ch:network}

The x86 version of Ulix has no network. This chapter is new. It is written from the bottom up, each layer small enough to read
and to test on its own against the real TCP/IP stack of the host (\texttt{make net-test}): the network card drivers (virtio-net
for qemu, GENET for the Raspberry Pi~4, which has not run on real hardware yet), Ethernet and ARP, IPv4 without fragments, ICMP echo
(\texttt{ping}), UDP, a small TCP with retransmission, a DHCP client, a DNS resolver, the configuration from the kernel command line, a network
thread that an interrupt wakes, and the system calls with which user programs reach all this. The goal is a telnet server;
until then the echo service shows how a server attaches to the stack.

IPv6 has a section of its own (\texttt{ip6.c}): the same layers with 128 bit addresses, Neighbor Discovery instead of ARP, and an address that the
router helps to make instead of DHCP. It stops after UDP; the section says why and what is left.

