\section{A resolver: turns a host name into an IP address (DNS)}
\label{src:dns.c}

People say \texttt{example.org}, IP wants 93.184.216.34. The \emph{Domain Name System} (RFC 1035) is the phone book: the
computer sends a question as a UDP datagram to its name server (port 53), the server sends the answer back. The address of the name
server is [[net_dns]]; DHCP or the command line ([[dns=]]) has put it there. A question and an answer have the same format:

\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
    | id (2) | flags (2) | questions (2) | answers (2) | authority (2) | additional (2) |       the header: 12 bytes
    | name | type (2) | class (2) |                                                           the question
    | name | type (2) | class (2) | time to live (4) | length (2) | data |   ...                the answers
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}

The \emph{id} is a number that we invent and the server copies into its answer, so that we can tell which question an answer
belongs to (and so that a forged answer has to guess it). In the flags we only set ``recursion desired'' (0x0100): ``please
ask the other servers for me, if you do not know it yourself.'' The \emph{name} is written as a sequence of labels, each with its
length in front, and ends with a zero byte: \texttt{example.org} is [[7 example 3 org 0]]. The \emph{type} 1 is ``A'' (the
IPv4 address), the \emph{class} 1 is ``IN'' (Internet). The data of an A record are the four bytes of the address.

The server does not repeat a name that it has written before: it puts a two byte \emph{pointer} instead. A byte that starts
with two one bits (0xC0) is the first half of the pointer, and the position (counted from the start of the message) is
in the 14 remaining bits. A name therefore ends with a zero byte or with a pointer, and that is all we need to know
to \emph{skip} it. We never have to read the names of the answers: the question was for one name, and if that name is an alias
(CNAME) the server sends the CNAME record first and then the A record of the real name; the first A record is what we want.

What the resolver does \emph{not} do: AAAA, MX and the other record types, several questions, TCP when the answer does not
fit into a datagram (a ``truncated'' answer is treated as no answer), a cache, and the search list of domains.

As in [[dhcp_run]], the caller says what is to happen while we wait ([[idle]]): the network thread sleeps, a program that asked
through the system call gives the CPU to the others. Nobody ever blocks the boot: the kernel itself never resolves a name.
If no answer comes within a second the question is sent again, then after two seconds, then after four.

