\section{IPv6: ICMPv6, Neighbor Discovery, autoconfiguration from router advertisements and UDP (see ip6.c)}
\label{src:ip6.c}

IPv6 is the successor of IPv4. The ideas of the layers stay the same (Ethernet below, ICMP, UDP and TCP above), but three things
change, and each of them shows in this file:

\begin{itemize}
\item The addresses have 128 bits (16 bytes), written as eight groups of four hex digits, with the longest run of zero groups
      replaced by [[::]]: [[fe80::5054:ff:fe12:3456]]. A number of 32 bits does not hold one, so addresses are byte arrays here.
\item There is no ARP and no DHCP broadcast. \emph{Neighbor Discovery} (RFC 4861) does the work of ARP, with ICMPv6 messages sent
      to a multicast address, and \emph{stateless address autoconfiguration} (SLAAC, RFC 4862) replaces DHCP: the router tells
      the computers which prefix the network has, and every computer makes its own address from the prefix and from its Ethernet
      address.
\item The header is simpler: fixed 40 bytes, no checksum of its own (the checksum of ICMPv6, UDP and TCP covers the addresses),
      no fragmentation by routers.
\end{itemize}

\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
    | version 6, traffic class, flow label (4) | payload length (2) | next header (1) | hop limit (1) |
    | source address (16) | destination address (16) | data ...
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}

``Next header'' is the protocol of the data (58 = ICMPv6, 17 = UDP, 6 = TCP). The \emph{link-local address} [[fe80::/64]]
is made from the Ethernet address (RFC 4291, ``modified EUI-64''): the six bytes are cut in the middle, [[ff:fe]] is put between
the halves, and one bit of the first byte is turned around (bit 1, ``universal/local''):

\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
    MAC  52:54:00:12:34:56   ->   interface identifier 5054:00ff:fe12:3456   ->   fe80::5054:ff:fe12:3456
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}

The computer has this address as soon as the card is there; it is only valid on the local network and needs no router. A
\emph{global address} is the 64 bits of a prefix that the router announces (for example [[fd00:1234:5678:9abc::/64]]) followed by
the same interface identifier.

The multicast addresses that matter here: [[ff02::1]] (all nodes), [[ff02::2]] (all routers) and the \emph{solicited-node
address} [[ff02::1:ffXX:XXXX]], where the last three bytes are those of the address that is asked for. A computer that asks
``who has fe80::...?'' sends to the solicited-node address of that address, so that only the few computers that
share these last 24 bits have to look at the message. On Ethernet a multicast address becomes the Ethernet address [[33:33]] followed by
the last four bytes of the IPv6 address. The card must let such frames in (the virtio card of qemu and the GENET of the Pi in
promiscuous mode do); [[ip6_wants_mac]] is the check that [[frame_input]] in [[net.c]] uses.

What is simplified, and said here once: there is \emph{no duplicate address detection} (a computer should ask for its own new address
before it uses it), the lifetimes of prefixes and routers are not watched (an address stays), there is one interface, no
fragmentation and no extension headers (a packet that has one is ignored), and neighbour entries do not age. Everything that is
there is exercised by the network test (\texttt{make net-test}) against the IPv6 stack of qemu's user-mode network.

\emph{TCP over IPv6 is left out on purpose.} [[tcp.c]] keeps the address of the other side as a 32 bit number in the connection, in the
lookup of connections, in [[emit]] and in the checksum. To take both kinds of address it would need a 16 byte address and a flag
in about ten places of a file that is easy to read now, and every one of them would be a small ``if IPv6'' in front of the
reader. That is where this chapter stops being simple, so the step ends after UDP. The seam is there: [[send6]] already puts a
checksum where a TCP segment has it (one more case), and [[pseudo_sum6]] is the pseudo header that TCP needs.

