void netlog (const char *line);

int netd_irq = 0;
int net_ssh = 0;                              // the SSH server is on: sshd_enable in /etc/rc.conf (default YES), and the system has one
int net_telnet = 0;                           // the telnet server is on: /etc/rc.conf says telnetd_enable="YES" (or says nothing)
static int running = 0, ticks_seen = 0;

static void wake (void) {
  thread_id t = front_of_blocked_queue (floppy_queue);
  if (t) deblock (t, &floppy_queue);
}

void netd_nudge (void) { wake (); }            // a process has written something for the telnet session: send it now, not in 50 ms

void netd_interrupt (void) {                  // the card has something: say so to the card, wake the thread
  net_dev_ack ();
  wake ();
}

void netd_tick (void) {                       // every 10 ms; the thread gets a nudge every 50 ms
  if (running && ++ticks_seen >= 5) { ticks_seen = 0; wake (); }
}

@
The network code does not write on the console after the boot: its messages would appear in the middle of what the user types,
and the boot would have to wait for them. A BSD system writes such messages into a log file. Ulix does the same, but only if
the administrator wants it: with [[netlog_enable="YES"]] in \texttt{/etc/rc.conf} (the default is [[NO]]) every message of
the network code (the link of the card, the DHCP request and its answer) is appended to \texttt{/var/log/net.log}, one line
each, with the time since the boot in seconds.

[[netlog]] only puts the line into a small ring buffer; it may be called from anywhere, also from the driver, and is quick.
The writing into the file is done by the network thread ([[netlog_flush]]), because it can sleep while the disk works. The
thread opens the file for every portion, appends to it and closes it again, so that the file is complete when the power goes
off (the disk buffers are written with [[buffer_sync]]). The directories \texttt{/var} and \texttt{/var/log} are made when they are
missing. If the buffer is full the new line is dropped: a log that blocks the network is worse than a log with a gap.

The thread starts its first flush while the boot is still running, and the boot code (thread 1, the idle thread) is at that moment
loading the first program from the same disk. That went wrong once in a few boots (a crash in the file system, and no log at all),
until the cause was found: thread 1 started its disk request without taking the disk lock, because it must not sleep, and so it
could start while the request of the network thread was on its way. The fix is in the disk driver (\texttt{glue.c}: thread 1 now takes
the lock by asking again and again), not here; the log may be written at any time.

<<netd.c: log>>=
static char log_ring[2048];
static int  log_in = 0, log_out = 0;                             // the buffer is a ring: written at log_in, emptied at log_out
static int  log_on = -1;                                         // -1: do not know yet, 0: off, 1: netlog_enable="YES"

void netlog (const char *line) {
  if (log_on < 0) log_on = rc_conf_enabled ("netlog_enable", 0);        // (off, unless /etc/rc.conf says YES)
  if (!log_on) return;
  char text[200];
  int n = sprintf (text, "[%us] %s\n", system_ticks / 100, line);
  if (n > (int)sizeof text - 1) n = sizeof text - 1;
  int used = (log_in - log_out + (int)sizeof log_ring) % (int)sizeof log_ring;
  if (n >= (int)sizeof log_ring - used) return;                          // no room: the line is lost
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { log_ring[log_in] = text[i];  log_in = (log_in + 1) % sizeof log_ring; }
}

static void netlog_flush (void) {
  static int directories = 0;
  while (log_out != log_in) {
    char chunk[256];  int n = 0;
    while (log_out != log_in && n < (int)sizeof chunk) { chunk[n++] = log_ring[log_out];  log_out = (log_out + 1) % sizeof log_ring; }
    if (!directories) { u_mkdir ("/var", 0755);  u_mkdir ("/var/log", 0755);  directories = 1; }      // (fails if they exist: fine)
    int fd = u_open ("/var/log/net.log", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, FOLLOW_LINK);
    if (fd < 0) continue;                                                // cannot write the log: the text is lost, the network goes on
    u_lseek (fd, 0, SEEK_END);
    u_write (fd, chunk, n);
    u_close (fd);
    buffer_sync (1);
  }
}

@
A cable that is plugged in \emph{after} the boot, or an address that the DHCP server did not give at the boot because
nobody answered: for a computer that is supposed to get its address from DHCP ([[ip=dhcp]]) that must not mean ``no address
for ever''. The boot itself does not ask the server (that could take seconds, and the console would show nothing but
a pause): the kernel only notes that the command line said [[ip=dhcp]], and the network thread does the asking, after the boot
and while the user already works. Every two seconds the thread asks the card whether there is a link ([[net_dev_link]]; the driver
of the Pi then also tells the MAC the speed that the PHY has agreed on). As long as there is a link but no address yet, it asks a
DHCP server, and if nobody answers it tries again after ten seconds. While it waits for the answer it sleeps, so the other threads run. What it does goes into the network log, never on the console.

While the thread waits for the answer it must really \emph{sleep}, as in its main loop ([[netd_sleep]]): it is woken by the interrupt
of the card or by the timer, and in between the other threads run. (With a plain [[arch_resign]] in a loop the thread stayed
runnable all the time and the boot, which was starting the first program, got no CPU for the seven seconds that the server may need.)

<<netd.c: link>>=
static void netd_sleep (void) {
  block (&floppy_queue, TSTATE_WAITFLP);
  arch_resign ();
}

static void netd_link (void) {
  static unsigned long long next = 0;
  static int told = 0;                                           // the log says that we are trying (and that nobody has answered yet)
  unsigned long long now = net_now_ms ();
  if (now < next) return;
  next = now + 2000;
  int up = net_dev_link ();
  if (!up || !net_dhcp_wanted || net_dhcp_done) return;
  if (!told) NETLOG ("net: asking a DHCP server for an address");
  if (dhcp_run (5000, netd_sleep) == 0) {
    net_dhcp_done = 1;  told = 0;
    NETLOG ("net: DHCP: address %d.%d.%d.%d, router %d.%d.%d.%d", net_my_ip >> 24, (net_my_ip >> 16) & 255, (net_my_ip >> 8) & 255, net_my_ip & 255,
            net_gateway >> 24, (net_gateway >> 16) & 255, (net_gateway >> 8) & 255, net_gateway & 255);
  } else {
    if (!told) { told = 1;  NETLOG ("net: no answer from a DHCP server yet, trying again every 10 seconds"); }     // (once: not every 15 seconds)
    next = net_now_ms () + 10000;
  }
}

@
IPv6 needs no asking of a server, only a question to the router: ``is anybody there who can tell me the prefix?'' (a router
solicitation, sent to all routers). The answer, a router advertisement, comes in like any other frame, through [[net_poll_once]] in the
loop of the thread, so there is nothing to wait for here, and the boot never waits. As long as there is a link but no router, the
thread asks again every four seconds, three times at most (RFC 4861 does the same); when the cable is pulled out and plugged in
again it starts anew. What arrives goes into the network log.

<<netd.c: link6>>=
static void netd_link6 (void) {
  static unsigned long long next = 0;
  static int asked = 0, told_router = 0, told_global = 0;
  unsigned long long now = net_now_ms ();
  if (now < next) return;
  next = now + 2000;
  if (!net_dev_link ()) { asked = 0;  return; }                  // no link: ask again when it is back
  char text[48], router[48];
  if (ip6_have_router && !told_router) { told_router = 1;  ip6_format (router, ip6_router);  NETLOG ("net: IPv6 router %s", router); }
  if (ip6_have_global && !told_global) { told_global = 1;  ip6_format (text, ip6_global);  NETLOG ("net: IPv6 address %s (from a router advertisement)", text); }
  if (ip6_have_router || asked >= 3) return;
  ip6_solicit ();
  asked++;
  next = now + 4000;
  if (asked == 1) NETLOG ("net: IPv6: asking for a router (router solicitation)");
}

@
<<netd.c: netd>>=
static void netd (void *arg) {
  for (;;) {
    int moved;
    do { moved = net_poll_once (); if (net_echo) moved |= echo_step (); if (net_telnet) moved |= telnet_step (); if (net_ssh) moved |= ssh_step (); } while (moved);       // everything that is there
    netd_link6 ();                                                                            // (IPv6: a router solicitation, until a router has answered)
    netd_link ();                                                                             // (every two seconds: link? an address yet?)
    netlog_flush ();                                                                          // (the network log, if there is something for it)
    netd_sleep ();                                                                            // then sleep until we are woken
  }
}

void netd_start (void) {
  netd_irq = net_dev_start_irq ();
  if (netd_irq) gic_enable (netd_irq);
  if (net_echo && echo_start () != 0) printf ("netd: cannot open port 7\n");
  net_telnet = rc_conf_enabled ("telnetd_enable", 1);              // on, unless /etc/rc.conf switches it off
  if (net_telnet && telnet_start () != 0) { printf ("netd: cannot open port 23\n"); net_telnet = 0; }
  net_ssh = ssh_start && rc_conf_enabled ("sshd_enable", 1);        // (a system built with TLS=0 has no SSH server at all)
  if (net_ssh && ssh_start () != 0) net_ssh = 0;
  arch_thread_create_high (netd, 0, "netd");                       // (a high thread number: see arch_thread_create_high)
  running = 1;
  printf ("netd: network thread started (%s)%s\n", netd_irq ? "woken by the interrupt of the card" : "polling every 50 ms", net_echo ? ", echo service on port 7" : "");
  if (net_telnet) printf ("netd: telnet server on port 23\n");
  if (net_ssh) printf ("netd: ssh server on port 22\n");
}

@
\section{The configuration file \texttt{/etc/rc.conf}}
\label{src:rcconf.c}

Which services a BSD system starts is written down in the file \texttt{/etc/rc.conf}, one setting per line:
\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
    telnetd_enable="YES"
    netlog_enable="NO"
    tls_enable="YES"
    sshd_enable="YES"
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}
Ulix does the same. A line has the form [[name="YES"]] or [[name="NO"]] (the quotes may be left out; everything behind a
[[#]] is a comment). The file is read once, when the network thread starts. The kernel asks for one setting at a time
with [[rc_conf_enabled]], and says what is to be assumed when the file or the line does not exist (the \emph{default}). For the
telnet server that is ``on'': a system without \texttt{rc.conf} behaves like one that says \texttt{telnetd\_enable="YES"}. To switch the
server off, write [[telnetd_enable="NO"]] into \texttt{/etc/rc.conf} as root.

The kernel reads the file with the small reader for the Minix file system ([[minixfs.c]]) that the ELF loader uses, because
no process exists yet. The file may be at most 1 KByte long; what lies behind that is ignored.

<<rcconf.c>>=
#include "net.h"

extern u32  minix_lookup (const char *path);
extern long minix_read (u32 ino_nr, u32 offset, void *buf, u32 len);

static char rc_text[1025];
static int  rc_loaded = 0, rc_size = 0;

static void rc_load (void) {                        // read /etc/rc.conf into rc_text, once
  rc_loaded = 1;
  u32 ino = minix_lookup ("/etc/rc.conf");
  if (ino == 0) return;                             // no such file: every setting has its default
  long n = minix_read (ino, 0, rc_text, sizeof rc_text - 1);
  if (n > 0) rc_size = (int)n;
  rc_text[rc_size] = 0;
}

@
A line is searched from its start: blanks first, then the name, then [[=]], then the value. A value that begins with Y, T, 1 or
\emph{ON} means yes (as in \texttt{rc.conf(5)} of the BSDs: YES, TRUE, ON, 1); one that begins with N, F, 0 or \emph{OFF} means no.
If the same name appears twice the last line counts, like in a shell script.

<<rcconf.c>>=
static int rc_is_yes (const char *v) {
  if (*v == '"') v++;
  char c = *v | 0x20;                               // (a lower case letter; a digit stays a digit for the test below)
  if (*v == '1' || c == 'y' || c == 't') return 1;
  if (*v == '0' || c == 'n' || c == 'f') return 0;
  if (c == 'o') return ((v[1] | 0x20) == 'n') ? 1 : 0;      // ON or OFF
  return -1;                                        // we do not understand it
}

int rc_conf_enabled (const char *variable, int fallback) {
  if (!rc_loaded) rc_load ();
  int answer = fallback;
  int length = 0;
  while (variable[length]) length++;
  for (const char *line = rc_text; *line; ) {
    const char *p = line;
    while (*p == ' ' || *p == '\t') p++;
    int i = 0;
    while (i < length && p[i] == variable[i]) i++;
    if (i == length && p[i] == '=') {               // the line begins with name=
      int v = rc_is_yes (p + i + 1);
      if (v >= 0) answer = v;
    }
    while (*line && *line != '\n') line++;          // next line
    if (*line == '\n') line++;
  }
  return answer;
}

@
\section{The telnet server}
\label{src:telnet.c}

\emph{Telnet} sends the keys of a terminal over a TCP connection and shows what the other computer writes: a terminal over the
network. Ulix already has ten virtual terminals (like the PC version of the book), and on each of the first five a program
\texttt{login} waits for a user name. The telnet server needs no new kind of terminal: it takes one of these, [[TELNET_VT]],
and acts as its keyboard and its screen.

\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
    keys:   TCP connection --> telnet_step () --> input buffer of terminal 4 --> login, shell, ...
    screen: login, shell, ... --> kputch () --> model of terminal 4 --> telnet_putch () --> ring --> TCP connection
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}

\paragraph{The way in.} What arrives over the connection is put into the keyboard buffer of terminal 4, just like the UART
does it for the console ([[telnet_input]] in [[arch_console.c]]). The programs on that terminal do not know the difference.

\paragraph{The way out.} [[kputch]] ([[arch_vt.c]]) writes every character that a process prints into the model of its terminal.
If the terminal is the one of the telnet session, it also calls [[telnet_putch]] here. That function must be quick and must not wait,
so it only puts the character into a \emph{ring buffer}: an array that is written at [[head]] and read at [[tail]]; both only count up,
and the place in the array is the count modulo the size. The network thread sends the ring over the connection ([[telnet_step]]). If
the ring is full, characters are lost; with 8 KByte that happens only when the other side does not read.

\paragraph{The telnet protocol} is almost nothing: bytes are sent as they are, except that the byte 255 ([[IAC]], ``interpret as
command'') begins a command of two or three bytes, which says that the sender \emph{will} or \emph{won't} do something, or asks the
other side to \emph{do} or \emph{not do} it (RFC 854). Two things are needed to make an ordinary telnet client behave like a terminal:
\begin{itemize}
\item The client must send every key at once and not collect a whole line: we say [[WILL SGA]] (suppress go-ahead).
\item The client must not show the keys itself: Ulix echoes what is typed (the program that reads the line does this, as on the
console), so we say [[WILL ECHO]]: ``I do the echo.''
\end{itemize}
All answers and all other commands of the client are read and ignored; for these two options that is good enough. A line ends with
CR LF (or CR NUL), which the server turns into one Enter. Output has to end its lines with CR LF as well, so each \verb|\n| becomes
two bytes. The byte 255 in the text is sent twice.

\paragraph{One session.} Only one person can use terminal 4 at a time. A second connection gets a message and is closed. When the
session ends (the client closes the connection), the programs on the terminal are ended, as if the line had been hung up
([[console_hangup]]); [[login]] then asks for a user name again, ready for the next connection. That the person is the right one
is checked by [[login]], with the password, as on the console. \emph{Telnet sends the password unencrypted:}
it is for a private network, a teaching system and trying things out.

@

The complete file is put together from these parts:

<<telnet.c>>=
<<telnet.c: includes>>

<<telnet.c: the ring>>

<<telnet.c: from the client>>

<<telnet.c: the session>>

<<telnet.c: telnet_step>>
@

<<telnet.c: includes>>=
#include "net.h"

#define TELNET_PORT 23
#define TELNET_VT   4                           // the terminal (Ctrl-T 5) on which the session runs: a login waits there

extern void telnet_input (int vt, unsigned char c);       // arch_console.c: a key from the session
extern void telnet_attach (int vt);                       // arch_console.c: the session takes over the terminal
extern void console_hangup (int vt);                      // arch_console.c: end the programs of the terminal
extern void arch_vt_telnet_attach (void);                 // arch_vt.c: send the screen of the terminal to the session

#define IAC   255                               // the telnet commands that we use
#define WILL  251
#define WONT  252
#define DO    253
#define DONT  254
#define SB    250                               // begin of a sub negotiation (ended by IAC SE)
#define SE    240
#define IP    244                               // "interrupt process": what some clients send for Ctrl-C
#define OPT_ECHO 1
#define OPT_SGA  3

int telnet_vt = -1;                             // the terminal of the session, -1 = no session
static tcp_conn *conn = 0;                      // the connection of the session

@
The ring buffer. [[telnet_putch]] is called by the process that prints, in the middle of a system call; it wakes the network thread
(only when the ring was empty before, to save work).

<<telnet.c: the ring>>=
static u8 ring[8192];
static volatile unsigned head = 0, tail = 0;    // written at head, read at tail; the place is the count modulo the size

static void put (u8 c) {
  if (head - tail >= sizeof ring) return;       // full: this character is lost
  ring[head % sizeof ring] = c;
  head++;
}

void ssh_putch (char c) __attribute__ ((weak));       // sshd.c: the terminal is used by an SSH session (a system built with TLS=1)

void telnet_putch (char c) {
  if (conn == 0) { if (ssh_putch) ssh_putch (c); return; }          // (the SSH server shares this terminal: see sshd.c)
  int was_empty = (head == tail);
  if (c == '\n') put ('\r');                    // a line ends with CR LF
  put ((u8)c);
  if ((u8)c == IAC) put (IAC);                  // the byte 255 in the text is sent twice
  if (was_empty) netd_nudge ();
}

@
What the client sends. A small state machine reads the bytes one by one: normally a byte is a key; behind [[IAC]] a command follows
whose bytes are skipped. [[after_cr]] remembers that the last key was Enter (CR), so that the LF or NUL behind it is not a second Enter.

<<telnet.c: from the client>>=
static int state = 0;                           // 0 = text, 1 = after IAC, 2 = after WILL/WONT/DO/DONT (one byte to skip), 3 = in SB ... SE, 4 = IAC inside SB
static int after_cr = 0;

static void from_client (u8 c) {
  switch (state) {
    case 1:                                     // the byte behind IAC
      state = 0;
      if (c == WILL || c == WONT || c == DO || c == DONT) state = 2;
      else if (c == SB) state = 3;
      else if (c == IP) telnet_input (TELNET_VT, 3);          // interrupt process: like Ctrl-C
      else if (c == IAC) telnet_input (TELNET_VT, IAC);       // the byte 255 as text
      return;
    case 2: state = 0; return;                  // the option that WILL/WONT/DO/DONT is about: ignored
    case 3: if (c == IAC) state = 4; return;
    case 4: state = (c == SE) ? 0 : 3; return;
  }
  if (c == IAC) { state = 1; return; }
  if (after_cr) {                               // the byte behind Enter: LF or NUL belongs to it
    after_cr = 0;
    if (c == '\n' || c == 0) return;
  }
  if (c == '\r') after_cr = 1;
  telnet_input (TELNET_VT, c);                  // (CR becomes Enter there)
}

@
Beginning and end of a session. At the beginning the client is told to send single keys and not to echo. The terminal is taken
over (its keyboard buffer is emptied) and its screen is sent, so that the client sees the prompt of [[login]] at once.

<<telnet.c: the session>>=
static void session_start (tcp_conn *c) {
  conn = c;
  head = tail = 0;
  state = 0;  after_cr = 0;
  telnet_vt = TELNET_VT;
  put (IAC); put (WILL); put (OPT_ECHO);        // "I echo": the client must not show the keys itself
  put (IAC); put (WILL); put (OPT_SGA);         // "send every key at once"
  telnet_attach (TELNET_VT);
  arch_vt_telnet_attach ();                     // paints the terminal into the ring
}

static void session_end (void) {
  conn = 0;
  telnet_vt = -1;
  console_hangup (TELNET_VT);                   // the programs of the session end, login starts again
}

int telnet_start (void) {
  return tcp_listen (TELNET_PORT);
}

@
The network thread calls [[telnet_step]] again and again. It accepts a new connection (or sends a busy message to it), reads what the
client has sent, and sends what is in the ring. The return value 1 tells the thread that something happened, so that it looks
again before it sleeps.

<<telnet.c: telnet_step>>=
int telnet_step (void) {
  static const char busy[] = "Ulix: this terminal is in use.\r\n";
  int moved = 0;

  tcp_conn *other = tcp_accept (TELNET_PORT);
  if (other) {
    if (conn || telnet_vt >= 0) { tcp_write (other, (const u8 *)busy, sizeof busy - 1); tcp_close (other); }      // (a session of telnet or ssh has the terminal)
    else session_start (other);
    moved = 1;
  }
  if (conn == 0) return moved;

  if (tcp_closed (conn)) { session_end (); return 1; }          // (reset by the other side: the pointer must not be used any more)
  u8 buf[256];
  int n = tcp_read (conn, buf, sizeof buf);
  if (n > 0) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) from_client (buf[i]);
    moved = 1;
  } else if (n < 0) {                                           // the client has closed the connection: so do we
    tcp_close (conn);
    session_end ();
    return 1;
  }

  while (tail != head) {                                        // send what the programs have written
    u8 chunk[256];
    int len = 0;
    while (len < (int)sizeof chunk && tail + len != head) { chunk[len] = ring[(tail + len) % sizeof ring]; len++; }
    int sent = tcp_write (conn, chunk, len);
    if (sent <= 0) break;                                       // the send buffer is full: the rest stays in the ring
    tail += sent;
    moved = 1;
  }
  return moved;
}

@
\section{System calls for the network (AArch64 only, numbers 602 and up; the programs use them through userland/arm64-extra.h)}
\label{src:net_syscalls.c}

Programs get raw ICMP sockets: they build ICMP messages themselves, the kernel puts them into IP packets ([[net.c]]). That is all
that ``ping'' needs. TCP and UDP sockets for programs come later (then telnet can be a program, too).

\begin{quote}\begin{verbatim}
    icmp_open ()           a socket number, or -1
    icmp_send (&m)         m.handle, m.ip, m.buf, m.len: send the ICMP message in m.buf to m.ip (0 = sent)
    icmp_recv (&m)         m.handle, m.buf, m.len (room), m.timeout_ms: wait for a message; m.ip = sender; returns its length, 0 = timeout
    icmp_close (handle)
    clock_us ()            microseconds since the start of the machine (wraps after 71 minutes; programs subtract two values)
    net_info (&i)          the interface: name, Ethernet address, IP address, netmask, router, name server (ifconfig, route)
    net_set (&s)           change address and netmask, or the router (only root; ifconfig, route)
    net_dhcp (&d)          ask a DHCP server and use what it says (only root; dhclient)
    dns_resolve (&q)       q.name: a host name; q.address: its IPv4 address; returns 0, -1 = no answer, -2 = no such name, -3 = failed (ping, host)
    net6_info (&i)         the IPv6 addresses: link-local, global (from a router advertisement) and the router (ifconfig)
    ping6 (&p)             p.address, p.size, p.timeout_ms: send one ICMPv6 echo request and wait for the answer; the time in microseconds, -1 = none
\end{verbatim}\end{quote}

A program that waits for an answer gives the CPU to the others in between ([[arch_resign]]), and looks into the network card each
time it gets the CPU back: for now there is no thread that does this for everybody (that needs an interrupt of the card).

